# Backend Conventions

## Layered Architecture Rules
- Controllers handle HTTP: parse input, call service, return response — no business logic here
- Services contain all business logic: validations, state transitions, budget calculation
- Repositories handle all database access — no raw queries outside repository layer
- Domain models/entities are plain objects with no framework dependencies

## User & Customer Split
- Creating a Customer must automatically create a linked User with role `CLIENT`
- User entity stores: name, email, password hash, roles
- Customer entity stores: CPF/CNPJ, name, phone, FK to User
- Default password = CPF (individual) or CNPJ (company), hashed with bcrypt (min cost 12)
- Deleting a Customer must also delete or deactivate the associated User

## RBAC — Role-Based Access Control
- Four roles: `ADMIN`, `ATTENDANT`, `MECHANIC`, `CLIENT`
- Users may hold multiple roles (many-to-many via `USER_ROLE`), except `ADMIN` which is exclusive
- **ADMIN**: unrestricted, including user management and role assignment
- **ATTENDANT**: customers, vehicles, service orders, budgets, reports — no user management
- **MECHANIC**: read-only service orders, add services/parts to service orders, status transitions only
- **CLIENT**: own service order status + own budget approve/reject; scoped by `customerId` in JWT
- Only `ADMIN` can create other `ADMIN` users
- Only `ADMIN` can change any user's roles
- Out-of-scope access must return HTTP 403
- Authorization middleware reads `roles` and `customerId` from JWT — must not make additional DB calls

## Service Order Status Transitions
- Status must only advance in the defined order: RECEIVED → IN_DIAGNOSIS → AWAITING_APPROVAL → IN_PROGRESS → COMPLETED → DELIVERED
- Rejecting a budget sets status to REJECTED and prevents any further transitions
- Always validate the current status before applying a transition; throw a domain error if invalid
- Log every status transition with structured fields: service_order_id, from_status, to_status, timestamp

## Stock Rules
- Always decrement stock inside a database transaction when associating a Part (Peca) to a service order (OS)
- Before decrementing, check quantity > 0; throw a domain error if out of stock
- Use SELECT FOR UPDATE (or equivalent pessimistic lock) to prevent race conditions on concurrent stock updates
- Never expose negative stock values; treat them as a data integrity violation

## Budget Calculation
- Budget = sum(service.unit_price) + sum(part.unit_price × quantity)
- Recalculate automatically whenever services or parts are added/removed from a service order
- The budget is read-only after the service order reaches IN_PROGRESS

## Validation Rules
- CPF: validate format (###.###.###-##) and digit verification algorithm
- CNPJ: validate format (##.###.###/####-##) and alphanumeric verification algorithm
- License plate: support both old format (ABC-1234) and Mercosul format (ABC1D23)
- All monetary values must be positive and stored as decimal/numeric (never float)
- Validate all input at the controller layer before passing to the service

## Authentication & Security
- All admin endpoints require a valid JWT Bearer token in the Authorization header
- Public endpoints: `POST /auth/login`, `GET /service-orders/:id/status` — no auth required
- CLIENT endpoints require JWT with role `CLIENT`; filter data by `customerId` from JWT
- Store passwords using bcrypt with a minimum cost factor of 12
- Never log CPF, CNPJ, passwords, or JWT tokens
- JWT expiry must be configurable via environment variable
- JWT payload must include `roles` (array) and `customerId` (when applicable)

## Error Handling
- Return structured error responses: `{ "error": "...", "code": "..." }`
- Use HTTP status codes correctly: 400 (validation), 401 (auth), 403 (forbidden), 404 (not found), 409 (conflict/stock), 422 (business rule), 500 (unexpected)
- Never expose stack traces or internal details in production responses

## Logging
- Use structured (JSON) logs with fields: timestamp, level, operation, entity_id
- Log levels: INFO for normal operations, WARN for business rule violations, ERROR for unexpected failures
- Mask or omit sensitive fields (CPF, CNPJ) in all log entries
